WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.120
Jeopardy! Jr.

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:05.500
Edition! We have the questions, do you have the answers?

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:28.900
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:29.760
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:30.900
And I'm Sean.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:39.920
You know, with Jeopardy!, there's usually three contestants, so this is a bit of a struggle to only have two people on the show today.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:47.880
It's actually just the host and one contestant, which I guess is more like who wants to be a millionaire.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:01:00.400
Well, what do you say we just no host this episode and we just both act as the contestants, if you will, and don't follow any proper means of lines of questioning?

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.780
What is no? What is no, Mike?

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.260
Okay, sounds good, sounds good.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.540
10,000 points from Mike.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.060
Well, I don't think you can steal points in a game of Jeopardy!.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.680
Well, there's no host to tell us that you can't, so.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.340
Oh, shit. Very good.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.840
You know, you bring up a good point, though, Sean.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.320
We are Jeopardy!

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.000
experts because we've already played this exact game.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.200
And we had more fun with it in our episode.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:31.800
If listeners didn't check out that episode, we actually made it into a game of Jeopardy!

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.180
And so it was a lot of fun and it was Sean versus Joe.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:42.800
And I was thinking this time around, instead of playing any kind of Jeopardy!, me being the Trebek or you being the...

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.760
Aaron Rodgers. Yeah, the Aaron Rodgers, who's also, he was a host, not a contestant.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:54.260
Yes. I thought we should just try and talk more about the actual game this time.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.180
Less theatrics and more just get to the nitty gritty.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.660
Yeah, you know, I hate when we try and do gimmicks.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:03.800
It's like we're being lazy.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.260
It's a crutch.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.040
I like the purity.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.180
No, I don't really care.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:09.680
Let's do it the way you want to do it.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.460
Great. Thanks so much for that.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.480
For those of you that are unaware, Jeopardy!

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.120
Junior Edition is literally just like playing Jeopardy!

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.080
on the TV show.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.200
The way that that's done is there are categories.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.360
And then they give you a question, a thing worded as like the answer.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.140
And then you have to respond with the question.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.680
So, Sean, what's a good example of something like that?

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.920
You just, well, you just did it wrong.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:40.700
You're supposed to, you asked the question first and now I'm giving you an answer.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.160
I should have said like, oh, no, you're right.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:53.900
Right. It's a little confusing when you post it as an actual game show because it sounds like, you know, even the back of the box, we have the questions.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.160
Do you have the answers?

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.560
But not necessarily true because you answer in a question.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.240
Yeah, I've never truly understood why Jeopardy!

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.960
was sort of like put together this way.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.420
But that's beyond the scope of this, right?

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.240
That's way beyond the scope of this.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.160
We're not here to debate network television policies.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:17.400
It's true. But yeah, like this is basically the same exact game that we played.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:26.160
But the sprites or like your little avatars are now shaped more like cartoon kids instead of cartoon adults.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.780
And the questions are like geared towards a lower age demographic.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.440
Yeah, that's the point.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:34.940
That's like the big difference here, right?

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:36.220
Is it's Jeopardy!

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.400
with supposedly easier questions because it's the junior edition.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:48.980
If you ever got at the board games like Monopoly Junior Edition or Trivial Pursuit Junior Edition, they're supposed to be geared towards kids.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:54.180
And I think one of the things we ran into when playing Jeopardy!

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.240
last time was that the questions didn't age that well because Jeopardy!

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:07.520
in general is asking questions to the people who are in their like 30s at the time of the making of the episode.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.300
So when you watch Jeopardy!

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.420
as a kid, you're like, what the hell?

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.980
How does everybody know these answers?

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.060
The guys on TV know the answers.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.340
My dad knows the answers.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.240
But you don't understand them at all.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.260
And then if you tune into an episode of Jeopardy!

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.820
now, you're going to realize, oh, it's just pop culture.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:29.700
Yeah. I always thought that there was like you needed to be a computer to be good at Jeopardy!

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:34.160
And like there's still a lot of like weird questions that even in, you know, this Jeopardy!

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.420
Junior, I was like, I have no idea what that even was.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:43.480
But it definitely is something that you sort of grow into and realize that it was easy all along.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:44.720
Like taxes.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.100
A little under the hood here for a minute.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:51.380
There are 348 categories in this game.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.800
But there are six categories in a round.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.720
So there's 58 groups of categories.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.720
Now, the categories aren't randomized.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.900
So you will always get the, you know, like if the category is the Flintstones,

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.260
that will always be grouped with the same other five categories.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:17.000
That is a very efficient way of making sure that you don't get the Flintstones as a category

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:20.420
as a possible chance every single time you boot up the game.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:26.260
Instead, your odds of getting a repeat game are just one in 58, which I like that.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:30.040
However, there are only 29 final Jeopardy!

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:32.600
questions, which checks out mathematically.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.240
58 divided by 2, 29.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:41.100
But if you think about it, it actually makes you more likely to run into repeat final Jeopardy!

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.040
questions sooner

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:46.380
because those aren't tied to the 58 groups.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.520
Okay, because that was sort of what I was going to ask.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.920
Like, so that's between regular and double Jeopardy!

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.680
Are those 58? Yes.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.180
And there's no like, exactly.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.780
The second round, the double Jeopardy!

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:04.080
round, that isn't like a harder group of questions that are singled off just for double Jeopardy!

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.460
They could be your potential first round as well.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.740
They just are mixing and matching these groups.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.280
So it's great as a way to like know that I've seen these categories before

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.240
and just hit the reset button to start a game.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.740
It's not as great after you've played a whole first round

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:24.880
and then you randomly get the chance of having a whole nother batch of categories you've already seen before.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:30.740
Yeah, so there's a maximum of 29 unique games, which, I mean,

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:35.280
if you're going to use this as a party game, it's probably enough.

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:41.200
But if you're going to be the weirdo that plays against the computer, like I imagine we did,

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:47.480
I don't think you're going to get a lot of lasting, a lot of staying power from this.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:49.940
And why do you think that is, Sean?

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:53.340
Well, because the games are relatively short.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:07:01.580
I think you kind of have to get through this game in something like 10 to 20 minutes, right?

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.600
Yeah, I'd say around that.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:04.640
It's definitely not.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:12.000
It's about the length of a regular episode because you have to spell out your answers.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.800
There are no choices.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.500
Yeah, there's less, you know, what do you do?

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:19.760
Do you have any weird stories about what you did in middle school?

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.680
You're just spelling the whole time.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:32.920
And that becomes an obstacle in itself if you are a little unsure of maybe how they want you to spell a certain thing.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:38.200
I will give a very unique example that I came across where I got the Fantastic Four category,

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.620
and they didn't ask me a single hard question.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:44.880
But they did ask me some questions that made me think for a minute of,

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:49.380
will they accept some variations of the answer or not?

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:54.660
So I tested it with, they asked what the Human Torch's real name is.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.980
So I tried John Flame, which sounds stupid, right?

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:03.320
You wouldn't call Peter Parker Pete Parker, but it's technically not wrong.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:07.440
However, they will only accept the exact answer.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.480
There is no room for Wiggle here.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.640
So like Pepe Le Pew, you can't just call him Pepe.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.000
Room for Wiggle.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:21.780
Yeah. I would say, okay, I didn't do too much testing,

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:26.980
but I want to say that there's at least like a handful of acceptable answers.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:32.740
Like Pepe Le Pew and just calling him Pepe sounds like you forgot the second half of his name.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:41.420
But if you're going to answer like who was the president after JFK, which is a question that I got,

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:45.500
I did Lyndon B.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.920
Johnson because I was afraid, but I would imagine they'd have to give you Lyndon Johnson.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.060
Or just Johnson.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.760
No. See, I think that should work though, right?

00:08:55.940 --> 00:09:02.800
Like in a perfect world, like they should be able to have like parsers for these kinds of things where like, you know, that's his last name.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.480
You would call George Bush Bush.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.300
Sure. I don't know.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:14.240
I feel like Jeopardy in spirit requires some amount of precision, right?

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:15.860
I don't know. No, I hear you.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:24.180
Just so you know, I'm only being antagonistic because of them choosing to make it about spelling and then leaving this kind of ambiguity to it.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:31.000
Something that you couldn't necessarily have in a real life game of Jeopardy where, you know, they don't ask you.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:35.980
And this is a silly example, but according to the song, the Rose of Texas is this color.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:37.660
If you say, what is yellow, right?

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:40.180
They don't ask you, can you spell yellow for me?

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:48.760
Well, I mean, just to confirm that, you know, I mean, I think maybe if you were on Jeopardy and the amount of times that you pronounce things in weird ways,

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:52.540
maybe that's just sort of simulating how you would perform.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.760
You know, if you said Yilo, I mean, they probably wouldn't give it to you.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.260
That's true. You've never said that, but you could have.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:02.360
Could have said it.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:03.420
I could have said any of these things.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.860
Also, though, it's not hard to spell a lot of these things.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:08.540
My point being that it's for kids.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.840
So maybe, you know, maybe they wouldn't necessarily know how to spell Chicago.

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:17.580
I'm just literally looking at the answers here.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:18.860
Just trying to caboose.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:21.080
Caboose would be a tough one for a kid to spell.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.880
I think a kid would use a K.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:25.940
I mean, I see what you mean.

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:28.860
Like, I might maybe there'd be two B's is what I'd say.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.260
Like, maybe they'd mix that up.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:32.780
Yeah, you're working with the gifted children.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.540
Let's talk about the quality of the questions then.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.400
So forget that you have to spell them right and everything like that.

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:50.860
What'd you just think of the questions, the categories in general?

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:57.640
I feel like for me, these are the kinds of questions we should be asking people who play in NES in 1989.

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:01.380
Like, most of your audience is probably kids or teens.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:08.560
This is like the right level of questioning so that it's not like inherently like super easy.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:11.100
Like, you know, the Looney Tunes category, for instance, right?

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:14.640
There there's no question about Bugs Bunny, right?

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:19.380
They're not just like teasing you with the most obvious things that like a Sesame Street level game would do.

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:23.940
But they're not challenging you if you don't know Looney Tunes either.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:35.300
Yeah. I did like the more like general knowledge ones that like the ones that are like plays on words and those sorts of things.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:47.600
But when they get into the stuff that is pop culture at the time, I get a little like this is this was the same in regular Jeopardy and it's to be expected in any kind of pop culture game.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:56.600
But there was an entire category for Captain Power, which I had never even heard of, but I imagine was popular in 1989.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:00.600
Have you heard of or watched Captain Power?

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:02.660
It was my first time hearing about it.

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:10.780
And I'll go even further, Sean, and just say that there are things that I know about, but don't know enough about them to answer questions like the Andy Griffith show.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:13.120
Yeah, I haven't watched the Andy.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:21.240
I'm aware of its existence, but don't ask me like anything about like where it takes place or who's who in the show or what the actor's name is.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:25.480
Yeah, I think it was Two's Company.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:28.500
Three's Company. No, it's not Three's.

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:30.060
It's about two people.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:34.840
I'll think of it in 10 minutes, but that sort of stuff.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:37.720
The TV show specific ones I could do without.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:46.300
But these were some very relaxing, easy, general trivia questions aside from those.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.160
Look, I'll put my point right now.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:52.480
Sean, you in your teenage years worked at a supermarket, correct?

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:53.920
I did do that.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:13:02.840
That's correct. So look, let's just go through the category and let's just show how easy but fair the game can be for children.

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:04.280
Okay? Okay.

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:09.160
This dairy item is usually sold in cartons by the dozen.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:11.860
Eggs. What is eggs?

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.460
There you go. What are eggs?

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:20.080
Yeah. The place where you pay for things you want to purchase.

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:25.600
Oh, that would be the cashier or the cash register?

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:31.160
You see, I guess we're not doing a good job proving our point here.

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:34.000
They're saying, what is the checkout counter?

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:36.200
That's a little too specific.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:39.300
And you see, cashier would be wrong in this scenario.

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:48.640
But I think that real life Jeopardy would understand the ambiguity of the question and be like, well, yeah, you go to the cashier to purchase things.

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:51.000
Huh. Yeah, I didn't really think about that.

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:54.900
A bakery bread item with a hole in the middle.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.680
Well, okay, there you go.

00:13:57.960 --> 00:14:00.100
I mean, that could be donuts or bagels.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.040
So which one is it, Sean?

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:06.020
You only get one. I'm going to have to go with donuts.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:07.400
It's a bagel.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:08.760
It's a bagel?

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:14.340
It's a bagel. The supermarket section where fruits and vegetables are found.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:16.460
Well, that's produce, Mike.

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:18.380
Okay, now here's the crazy thing, though, right?

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.480
That was the $400 question.

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:25.380
And yet the checkout counter is the $200.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:28.780
Yeah, it's like, you know, there was no structure.

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:31.260
Like the $500 question is supposed to be the hardest.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:34.400
But I didn't ever really come across that.

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:38.120
I mean, yeah.

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:44.340
Yeah. There's, and some of them, it's kind of not, it's not very consistent.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:48.480
Sometimes I found the $200 to be the most hard.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:52.600
And maybe even sometimes they'll just throw like the hardest to be the double jeopardy.

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:55.360
And sometimes it was very easy.

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:58.400
But yeah, I'm not really sure who proofread all these.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.240
All right. Last question in the category, Sean.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:05.260
The counter that sells cheese, cold cuts, and prepared foods.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:07.380
Where is New Delhi?

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:09.840
Delhi. No, yeah, yeah.

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:10.980
We'll accept either.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:15.060
Delhi technically has an H in it, though.

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:16.880
So the game would have ruled you wrong.

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:20.700
Absolutely. Spelling, man.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:21.920
It's tricky. I'm telling you.

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:31.240
So we're agreeing that the quality of the questions is pretty good and that the categories are definitely geared towards kids, which is important.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:34.300
With the exception of like one per group, maybe.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:39.720
Like there's just one thing where it's like either that's general knowledge or just knowledge that a kid wouldn't have.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:40.700
Right? Yeah.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:46.640
What about the computers, though?

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:49.760
You've got two other computer contestants.

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:54.260
If you don't play with friends, how are they doing in this game?

00:15:55.260 --> 00:16:04.220
Terribly. I feel like I don't know if if they've just toned down the difficulty because I was always playing on hard.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:14.180
And I would imagine that that means that you have less time on the buzzer on average and they are more likely to get questions correct.

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:19.720
And I had situations where I just let I just let them have it.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:26.300
I didn't even try to mean sometimes it was for things like the Captain Power category where I didn't know anything anyway.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:29.040
Just like the computer is going to have this category.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.780
So I'm just going to let them answer.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:34.680
And they just let the clock run out.

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:40.900
And then a second one in the same category that the same person person picked.

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:43.400
Then they let the clock run out.

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:45.100
